## What Happens After Publish

```
Published Class
      │
      ├──→  Neo4j gets OntologyClass node + Property nodes
      │
      ├──→  Term records updated: status UNASSIGNED → PROMOTED
      │         + linked to canonical property
      │         + source_class set to Consumer
      │
      ├──→  Class Reference properties become
      │         relationship edges in Neo4j
      │         (Consumer)-[:HAS_ADDRESS]->(Address)
      │
      └──→  Embeddings generated for
                class description
                each property (canonical + all synonym terms)
                ready for semantic search in VKG
```

---

## Overall State Machine for a Term

```
EXTRACTED
   │
   │  appears in search results
   ▼
CANDIDATE  ──── user ignores ────→  EXTRACTED (stays)
   │
   │  user selects + assigns to class
   ▼
PROMOTED
   │  canonical name chosen
   │  range defined
   │  class assigned
   ▼
PUBLISHED  ──── class publishes ──→  ACTIVE in ontology
   │
   │  future: class deprecated
   ▼
DEPRECATED
```

---

## Key Design Principles

**1. Terms are the entry point, not properties**
You never define a blank property form. You always start from a real extracted term.

**2. Semantic search drives everything**
Parent class lookup, range class lookup, synonym lookup — all semantic, never dropdown-only.

**3. Class Reference = automatic relationship**
The builder handles the pivot from property space to graph edge space transparently.

**4. Coverage visibility**
You can always see what % of your extracted terms have been promoted. The library shrinks as the ontology grows.

**5. Draft → Publish gate**
Nothing hits Neo4j until you explicitly publish. Lets you iterate freely.

